Answer: The area $A$ of a disk of radius $r$ is given by $A(t)=\pi r^2(t)$, so the rate of increase of the area for a disk of radius $r$ is (by definition) $$A'(t)=\frac{dA}{dt}=2 \pi r(t)\frac{dr}{dt}.$$ Plugging in $A'(t)=2\pi$  cm$^2$s$^{-1}$ directly gives you $dr/dt=1/r(t)=1/6$ cm/s $\simeq0.16666$ cm/s. 
